{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '—|\\\\|'> /dev/null; awk 'length(\"'$0)>80 {print NR \":\" length($0) \":\" $0}'\"' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md; sed -n '1,260p' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md\"","aggregated_output":"# yin.vm de Bruijn VM\n\nStatus: design; not implemented\n\nThis document specifies a second executable path for `yin.vm`.  The existing\npath lowers the named Universal AST to `:yin.code/*` and executes that image.\nThis design lowers the derived de Bruijn records to a separate instruction\ndimension and executes that image beside the named path.  It does not replace,\nreinterpret, or mutate the named path.\n\n## 1. Objective and non-negotiable invariants\n\nThe objective is to execute a deterministic linear image derived from the\nde Bruijn projection.  For a fully expanded program, the following observable\nbehaviour is the equivalence contract:\n\n1. Values and errors are equal under the existing semantic comparison.\n2. Evaluation order, effects, stream outcomes, and store transitions are\n   equal.\n3. Tail calls have the same termination and continuation behaviour.\n4. `current-continuation`, `park`, and `resume` preserve the same local\n   machine state and wake behaviour.\n5. `gensym`, macros after expansion, primitives, FFI, and stream operations\n   have the same results.\n6. Free names resolve in the existing order: environment, store, primitives,\n   then module registry.  The de Bruijn environment supplies positional bound\n   values; a named free environment supplies the first lookup tier.\n\nThis is an observational contract, not an instruction-for-instruction one.\nBinder spelling, source eids, temporary ids, program counters, allocation\nidentity, and `:yin.code/source` provenance are not promised to agree.  Stack\ntraces and debugger locations may identify a de Bruijn node hash rather than a\nnamed source entity.  The named AST remains the source of truth for rendering,\nsource maps, and diagnostics that require names.  Projection is a compile-time\ninput and must already be fully macro expanded; an unexpanded macro is a\ndiagnostic, not a runtime feature.\n\nThe implementation obeys the datom.world invariants:\n\n- all machine state is explicit data;\n- an instruction only classifies or advances explicit state;\n- no callback is retained or invoked by the VM;\n- stores and continuations are returned as explicit transitions;\n- the VM is an interpreter above the stream and datom layers;\n- code is treated as data, not as an assumed graph or host closure.\n\nThe projection and its fingerprint are inputs to this design.  This design\ndoes not change the projection, its descriptor, its canonical encoder, or its\nhash rules.\n\n## 2. Instruction dimension and coexistence\n\nThe VM uses a new published dimension, `:yin.debruijn.code/*`.  Existing\n`:yin.code/*` semantics remain byte-for-byte and behaviour-for-behaviour\nunchanged.  A new dimension is preferable to adding positional operations to\nthe named instruction set:\n\n- the projected image has a different identity and a different validator;\n- old loaders cannot accidentally accept an instruction with a new calling\n  convention;\n- the projected code can be cached by the projection fingerprint;\n- the existing code validator and semantic VM remain reusable consumers of\n  their own dimension.\n\nThe new descriptor is itself content addressed.  Its slots describe a code\nsegment, instruction operation, scalar operands, ordered child/body\nreferences, and the projection fingerprint from which the segment was made.\nThe descriptor hash is the domain separator for code-image encoding.  A code\nimage hash is derived from the canonical instruction bytes and descriptor\nhash; it is distinct from the source projection fingerprint, although the\nlatter is its stable source identity.\n\nThe canonical operations are the named linearizer's operations with the\nlexical operations made explicit:\n\n    :load-bound    [frame-depth position]\n    :load-free     name\n    :literal       value\n    :closure       arity body-ref\n    :call          argc tail?\n    :jump          label\n    :branch        then-label else-label\n    :return\n    :primitive     name operands\n    :stream-op     operation operands\n    :store-op      operation operands\n    :continuation  operation operands\n    :ffi-call      name argc\n\nThe exact scalar slot types and opcode list are published by the new\ndescriptor in B1.  `:yin.code/*` is not extended with these operations.  A\nshared, private validation helper may be introduced only if it leaves both\npublished dimensions unchanged.\n\n## 3. Lowering projected records\n\n### 3.1 Input and deterministic traversal\n\nThe lowerer receives a complete projected record set and its root\nfingerprint.  It first verifies that the root exists, every referenced record\nexists, and every record has the descriptor slots required by its tag.  It\nthen walks the record DAG in the same left-to-right order used by the named\nlinearizer.  The output instruction vector and all labels are deterministic\nfunctions of the records, root, descriptor hash, and lowering version.\n\nThe lowerer emits lambda bodies out of line.  A closure instruction contains\nthe lambda arity and a body reference.  The body starts with a frame for its\narguments and ends with `:return`.  Application evaluates its operator first,\nthen operands left to right, then performs the call.  `if` evaluates its\ncondition and exactly one selected branch.  Stream and VM primitives retain\nthe ordering and outcome discipline of the named linearizer.\n\nTail position is recomputed from syntax, not copied from a dropped\n`:yin/tail?` fact.  The root expression, a lambda body, and the selected arm\nof a tail-position `if` are tail positions.  An application in such a\nposition receives `tail? true`; all other applications receive `false`.  The\nsame structural propagation is used for the stream application forms that\nthe named linearizer marks as tail calls.\n\n### 3.2 Shared DAG nodes and lexical context\n\nHash-consing is a storage optimisation, not an instruction-sharing promise.\nThe first implementation expands each occurrence of a shared node at its use\nsite.  This is deliberately conservative: `[depth position]` is relative to\nthe lexical frame stack at the use site, and a generic shared code block would\nneed a separately specified closure or parameter convention.  Expansion\npreserves the projected indices without inventing a new environment.\n\nThe lowerer may memoise a fully emitted occurrence for identical\n`[node-hash, lexical-context, tail-context]` during one run, but the memo must\nnot alter output or identity.  A future block-sharing optimisation may emit a\nnode once only after its calling convention is specified and different\nlexical contexts are proven equivalent.  It is not B0 through B5 scope.\n\n### 3.3 Provenance\n\nThere is no source-to-projected index in the current projection.  Instructions\ntherefore do not carry `:yin.code/source`.  A debug-only\n`:yin.debruijn.code/node-hash` may identify the projected record that generated\nan instruction; it is excluded from code identity and is not a source map.\nNamed source locations remain available only through the named AST path or an\nowner-approved optional lineage index.\n\n## 4. VM state and execution\n\n### 4.1 Frames and closures\n\nThe machine state is plain data:\n\n    {:segment code-image\n     :pc pc\n     :frames [frame ...]\n     :free-env named-map\n     :stack operand-vector\n     :continuation continuation-data\n     :store store\n     :status status}\n\nA frame is a positional vector.  Frame zero is the innermost frame.  A\nclosure captures a persistent copy of the frame stack visible at creation and\nthe code body reference.  On call, the VM pushes a new frame made by\n`bind-params`: parameters are positional, missing arguments become `nil`, and\nextra arguments are discarded.  Duplicate named parameters no longer matter\nat runtime because the projection has already chosen rightmost-wins while\nconstructing the projected indices.\n\n`:load-bound [d p]` reads position `p` from frame `d`.  The loader and lowerer\nguarantee that the pair is in range before execution.  `:load-free name`\nuses the existing resolution order with `free-env` as the environment tier,\nthen the existing store, primitive table, and module registry.  No host map is\nused for bound locals.\n\n### 4.2 Calls, returns, and continuations\n\nCall and return preserve the existing operand-stack and frame semantics.  A\ntail call reuses the current call continuation where the named VM would do so;\nit does not retain an unbounded return frame.\n\n`current-continuation` reifies code identity, program counter, frame stack,\nfree environment, operand stack, continuation chain, and the relevant store\nview.  `park` returns the same explicit blocked outcome as the existing VM;\n`resume` restores that data and continues at its saved point.  Continuations\nare local machine values in this phase.  The universal continuation format is\nthe intended cross-engine representation, but its design is proposed and\ndeferred, so cross-host or remote continuation transport is not promised.\n\nThe implementation reuses `yin.vm.engine` resolution, primitive, stream,\nstore, and FFI helpers where their contracts are already data-oriented.  It\ndoes not change an existing protocol or add an operation to an existing VM.\nThe sibling namespace `yin.vm.debruijn-vm` may implement the already published\nVM protocols without adding protocol methods; otherwise its public `run`,\n`step`, and `load` functions are sufficient.\n\n## 5. Validation and failure rules\n\nThe projected reader intentionally remains an opaque record reader and does\nnot know the lexical context of a bound pair.  Scope validation belongs at the\nlowering boundary, where each occurrence has its frame-stack context.  B2\nmust reject, as a terminal diagnostic, every pair that is not two nonnegative\nintegers, every depth greater than or equal to the current frame count, and\nevery position greater than or equal to the referenced frame arity.  A lambda\nbody establishes its own frame before its descendants are checked.\n\nThe loader repeats cheap operand-shape checks on the linear image.  An\ninvalid image produces `:invalid-scope`, `:invalid-code`, or the published\nmalformed-record diagnostic, never a host index exception or an implicit\nexecution attempt.  A projected record may remain stored and queryable even\nwhen it cannot be lowered.\n\n## 6. Implementation phases\n\nEach phase is independently reviewable and shippable.  The implementation\nmust use a different model family for review and the GPT architect for signoff.\n\n### B0: contract and fixtures\n\n    New: docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md\n    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_vm_contract_test.cljc\n    Existing edits: none\n\nFreeze the descriptor vocabulary, state shape, error rules, and differential\nfixtures.  Completion requires fixtures for every projected node tag,\nshadowing, free lookup, duplicate parameters, tail calls, and an invalid\nscope pair.\n\n### B1: code dimension and validator\n\n    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc\n    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc\n    Existing edits: none\n\nPublish the `:yin.debruijn.code/*` descriptor, canonical opcode slots, image\nencoding, and deterministic code hash.  Completion requires round-trip\nencoding, malformed-record diagnostics, and identical bytes on JVM, Node, and\nDart.  The existing `yin.vm.code` validator remains unchanged.\n\n### B2: projected lowerer\n\n    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc\n    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize_test.cljc\n    Existing edits: none\n\nLower a projected graph to the new image, validate scope, recompute tail\nposition, preserve evaluation order, and expand shared occurrences.  Completion\nrequires deterministic output, all node tags, adjacent roots, and a test that\nthe memo can be disabled without changing the image.\n\n### B3: frame VM kernel\n\n    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_vm.cljc\n    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_vm_test.cljc\n    Existing edits: none\n\nImplement loading, stepping, frames, closures, positional loads, free loads,\ncalls, returns, literals, branches, and store operations.  Completion requires\nthe existing semantic fixtures to agree on values, errors, and stores for\npure programs.  No change is made to `yin.vm.semantic`, `yin.vm.engine`, or\n`:yin.code/*`.\n\n### B4: effects, streams, and continuations\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
